# OmniChannel E-Commerce Platform — Project Rules

## Tech Stack
- Backend: Express.js + TypeScript + Mongoose (MongoDB)
- Frontend: React + Vite + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS v4
- Real-time: Socket.io
- Cache/Sessions: Redis
- Payments: Stripe
- File Storage: AWS S3
- Email: SendGrid
- Containerization: Docker Compose

## Architecture Rules
- Monorepo: /client and /server are separate workspaces
- Server structure: src/{config,routes,controllers,models,services,middleware,utils}
- Every route needs: route file → controller → service layer (business logic stays in services, NOT controllers)
- All DB operations use Mongoose models — never raw MongoDB queries
- Use MongoDB sessions for transactions (stock reservation, order creation)

## Code Style
- TypeScript strict mode — no `any` types without explicit comment explaining why
- Use async/await everywhere — no .then() chains
- All API responses follow this format: { success: boolean, data?: T, message?: string, error?: string }
- Error handling via a global error middleware — never send raw errors to client
- Environment variables only through process.env, validated at startup

## Authentication
- JWT in HTTP-only cookies (not localStorage)
- Access token: 15min, Refresh token: 7d rotated and stored in Redis
- 4 roles: superAdmin, vendorManager, warehouseStaff, customer
- Role-based access via middleware that checks req.user.role

## Database Conventions
- All timestamps: { createdAt, updatedAt } via Mongoose timestamps option
- Soft delete with `isDeleted` boolean field, never hard-delete
- Inventory uses `reservedQty` field for checkout stock holding (15-min TTL)
- Indexes on all queryable fields + compound indexes for common queries

## UI Reference
- Visual mockups for all pages are in docs/ui/ folder — see docs/ui/README.md for the screen map
- When building any UI page, check the matching mockup image in docs/ui/ first
- Follow the visual direction, layout hierarchy, and component grouping shown in the mockups
- The mockups show a light enterprise-commerce theme: off-white canvas, white cards, dark navy text, cobalt blue primary actions, mint green success states, amber for attention/warnings
- Customer pages use full storefront header; admin/vendor/warehouse pages use a persistent dark-navy sidebar
- Preserve the status labels shown in mockups — they correspond to the order state machine: pending_payment, processing, confirmed, shipped, delivered, cancelled
- The mockups are conceptual references for layout, component grouping, and visual states — use real backend data and validation from the development plan PDF when implementing

## Reference Document
- Full development plan with schemas, API specs, and roadmap: docs/OmniChannel_ECommerce_Development_Plan.pdf
- When building any feature, consult the PDF first for field-level schema details